# Pending human-applied patch — issue #679
#
# WHAT: adds a guard deny rule for raw-git-transport `git push` invocations whose effect can
#       reach the repository's default branch — refspec destinations naming it (bare name,
#       `HEAD:main`, `feature:main`, the `:main` delete form, `refs/heads/main`), `git push
#       --delete <remote> <default>`, and `git push --mirror`/`--all` (either can carry the
#       default branch regardless of other args). `--force`/`-f`/`--force-with-lease` are
#       subsumed by the same refspec-destination check, not special-cased. Active ONLY when the
#       trusted spawn env `SAPWOOD_DEFAULT_BRANCH` is set (worker.ts wires it — see this issue's
#       ordinary-path PR); unset leaves the rule inactive, so non-worker `claude` sessions are
#       unaffected. Also adds guard.test.ts's BLOCK/ALLOW matrix for the rule (below), which the
#       applying human's own `npm test` (post-apply) exercises.
#
# WHY: Category C already enforces producer≠merger at the `gh` layer (`gh pr merge` etc denied).
#       It has no `git push` handling at all, so a worker leg holding `Bash(git *)` — with host
#       credentials at L0, or the #606 L1 deploy key, which docs/security.md itself notes "CAN
#       still `git push` directly to an unprotected default branch, bypassing the review gate
#       entirely via raw git transport" — can run `git push origin HEAD:<default-branch>`: a
#       merge-equivalent that skips gate①/gate② on any repo without branch protection. Branch
#       protection remains the backstop of record (DR #616) — this is engine-side defense-in-
#       depth AT the guard's own sanctioned enforcement point, an extension of the existing
#       Category C mechanism, never a replacement for the backstop.
#
# WHY IT IS A PATCH AND NOT A COMMIT: guard.ts / the guard hook wiring is human-merge-only
#       (CLAUDE.md "Non-negotiables"; canonical list docs/security.md "Human-merge-only paths").
#       An autonomous worker's write to guard.ts/guard-hook.ts/guard.test.ts is denied at the
#       tool level:
#         BLOCK [write-path] guard/reviewer/merge-driver source is human-merge-only
#       The guard constrains Claude tool calls, never a human's editor.
#
# APPLY (from the repo root), then delete BOTH this file AND the apply-check test that exists
# only to prove it in CI pre-apply (its job is done the moment the patch below lands for real —
# leaving it in place afterward would either dangle on a deleted patch file or, if made to
# tolerate that, silently stop verifying an accidental deletion of the real deny rule):
#       git apply docs/patches/679-guard-default-branch-push.patch
#       git rm docs/patches/679-guard-default-branch-push.patch
#       git rm engine/src/guard/patch-679-apply-check.test.ts
#       npm --workspace engine test    # exercises the new BLOCK/ALLOW rows this patch adds
#
# VERIFIED: this diff applies cleanly to main at ada7d4e (`git apply --check`, this issue's own
#       CI step — see engine/src/guard/patch-679-apply-check.test.ts) and, applied + `npm test`
#       in a disposable clone, the full engine suite is green (4753 passed / 4 pre-existing
#       skips / 0 failed), including every BLOCK/ALLOW row this patch's own guard.test.ts hunk
#       adds. If guard.ts/guard-hook.ts/guard.test.ts have moved or changed since ada7d4e, the
#       hunks still locate by context (offset-tolerant); re-check the result and re-run the
#       suite regardless.
